<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Satto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1350">1350</a>–<a href="/years/1395">1395</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Satto was king of Chūzan on Okinawa from 1350 to 1395, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a> castle. In 1372 he answered the call of the Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> and opened tribute relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, gaining access to Chinese trade and goods, and he also sent missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. During his reign Chinese settlers arrived at Kume near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a>, laying the foundations of the Ryukyu Kingdom's maritime trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the official histories of the Ryukyu Kingdom, Satto was born to a peasant family in Jana, a village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a>. With his wife's help he bought large amounts of iron tools from Japanese merchants and gave them to the peasants, which won him acclaim and let farming flourish. He took control of Urasoe around the death of King Tamagusuku, and when Tamagusuku's son Seii died, local rulers refused to enthrone Seii's heir and Satto took the kingdom of Chūzan, purportedly with wide popular support. Whether Chūzan and its rivals Hokuzan and Sannan existed as territorial states is debated by scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Ming official Yang Zai returned to Chūzan in 1372 with a decree asking for tribute, and Satto was given the title King of Ryukyu, though he probably controlled only a small part of central Okinawa around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a>, Shuri and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a>. In 1374 he sent his brother Taiki to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> sent back textiles, ceramics, books, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> royal seal and documents recognising him as king. From 1382 the Ming also recognised Hokuzan and Nanzan, which had to trade through Naha; between 1372 and 1398, 57 tributary missions went from Okinawa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In 1389 Satto sent an embassy to the new Joseon court with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><p>He is credited with founding Gokoku-ji, the second Buddhist temple on Okinawa, in 1384, and the earliest parts of Shuri Castle may date from his reign. A cash coin, the Chūzan Tsūhō, was allegedly minted under him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuzan-seikan">Chūzan Seikan</a> describes a good king who grew decadent, and the official histories say heaven cursed him and he died after touching a poisonous snake. He has no known tomb. His purported son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunei">Bunei</a> was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a> in 1405, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sho-dynasty">First Shō dynasty</a>. The name may be a title based on the Korean word sado, a local official, and the historian Gregory Smits suggests that Satto was a seafaring merchant from Goryeo; Smits also describes his later biographies as hagiographical.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Satto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1372 King <b>Satto</b> of Chuzan answered an invitation from the Hongwu Emperor and sent tribute to Ming China, and soon all three principalities were trading with the Ming court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Satto</b> opened tribute relations with Ming China in 1372, gaining access to Chinese trade and receiving skilled immigrants who settled at Kume village near Naha.</blockquote><a href="/state/chuzan">Chuzan</a></li></ul></section>
